WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.840
Does a taste field in cortex do anything?

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.140
And can a mouse know a taste, but stop wanting it?

3
00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:15.650
In anaesthetized mice, one study reported separate fields for sweet, bitter, umami

4
00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:16.870
and sodium.

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.523
Fields would matter more if they did something.

6
00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:23.723
Peng and colleagues made the fields respond to light, in awake mice.

7
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.907
Light on the sweet field drove attraction.

8
00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:29.567
Light on the bitter field drove aversion.

9
00:00:30.527 --> 00:00:31.587
That is the value half.

10
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:37.167
To test identity, they trained mice to respond to bitter, and to hold back for

11
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:38.167
sweet.

12
00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:41.107
Why set the task against the mouse's own preference?

13
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.320
So the mouse cannot just follow its liking.

14
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.780
But it could still learn a rule, such as respond to the unpleasant one.

15
00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:54.410
So the team added controls.

16
00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:59.230
Trained mice judged new sweet and bitter chemicals with similar accuracy.

17
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:04.230
In one version, low salt, which mice like, shared the bitter answer.

18
00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:06.750
Liking alone could not solve that.

19
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.990
Blocking either field impaired recognition of its taste.

20
00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.530
Recognition recovered after the drug washed out.

21
00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:16.953
Is there a tidy map, then?

22
00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:23.513
That is disputed. In alert, licking mice, another study found distributed responses.

23
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:29.107
Next, Wang and colleagues traced the two fields to different parts of the amygdala.

24
00:01:30.267 --> 00:01:31.787
Then they silenced the amygdala.

25
00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.140
What survives: recognizing the taste, or the pull toward the sweet field?

26
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:44.503
Recognition stayed.

27
00:01:45.443 --> 00:01:47.843
The mice still reported sweet and bitter correctly.

28
00:01:48.623 --> 00:01:51.063
But the attraction to sweet-field light was abolished.

29
00:01:55.166 --> 00:02:00.266
So identity and value can be pulled apart, in these assays and with this silencing

30
00:02:00.266 --> 00:02:01.266
method.

31
00:02:01.616 --> 00:02:02.916
The pattern is simple.

32
00:02:03.876 --> 00:02:07.516
Manipulate one stage, measure two behaviours, and see which one moves.

33
00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:10.466
Value also changes with need.

34
00:02:11.346 --> 00:02:15.826
A sodium-depleted animal drinks salt concentrations it would normally reject.

35
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:18.260
The salt is the same.

36
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.520
What changes, in these assays, is how much the animal wants it.

37
00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:28.433
Keep the scope. These are mouse assays.

38
00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:32.373
They do not show that value is computed only in the amygdala.

39
00:02:33.516 --> 00:02:38.176
In decerebrate rats, brainstem circuits still support innate acceptance and

40
00:02:38.176 --> 00:02:40.956
rejection. And learning shapes value too.

41
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:47.310
In these mouse experiments, silencing the amygdala removed the attraction and spared

42
00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.230
trained recognition.
